::ENG3_U05_L01_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Draft: The empty market symbolizes civic memory it still holds traces of public life. Which revision fixes the run-on? {
~The empty market symbolizes civic memory, it still holds traces of public life.#This is a comma splice.
=The empty market symbolizes civic memory because it still holds traces of public life.#Correct. The sentence is complete and clearly connected.
~Because the empty market symbolizes civic memory and public life.#This is a fragment.
~The empty market symbolizing civic memory it still holds traces.#This is not a correct sentence.
}
::ENG3_U05_L01_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Draft: Because the station clock has stopped. Which revision makes the fragment complete? {
~Because the station clock has stopped and memory.#This remains incomplete.
~The station clock has stopped because.#This is not a complete thought.
~Stopped clock because memory is shown.#This is awkward and incomplete.
=The stopped station clock suggests that memory has become frozen in place.#Correct. This is complete and analytical.
}
::ENG3_U05_L01_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.W.04] Draft: The author does a good job with the symbol. Which revision is most precise? {
=The author develops the symbol by connecting the locked gate to restricted possibility.#Correct. This is precise and analytical.
~The author does well with symbolism.#This is still vague.
~The symbol is good and important.#This is too general.
~The author has a symbol that works.#This is vague.
}
::ENG3_U05_L01_Q04::[MLA.ENG3.R.10] Source sentence: Public spaces can preserve memory when they show traces of repeated community use. Which paraphrase is clearest? {
~Public spaces preserve repeated traces when memory uses community.#This is unclear.
~Community use is repeated by public spaces and traces.#This is not standard English.
=A public place can suggest shared memory when it shows signs that people used it over time.#Correct. This accurately paraphrases the source.
~Public places are always better at memory than private places.#This adds an unsupported comparison.
}
::ENG3_U05_L01_Q05::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Draft: The source explains the image, the passage gives evidence. Which revision fixes the comma splice? {
~The source explains the image, the passage gives evidence.#This remains a comma splice.
~Because the source explains the image and the passage gives evidence.#This is a fragment.
=The source explains the image, and the passage gives evidence.#Correct. The comma plus coordinating conjunction correctly joins the sentences.
~The source explaining the image and the passage giving evidence.#This is a fragment.
}
::ENG3_U05_L01_Q06::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Context: Choose the sentence that uses academic vocabulary correctly. {
~The symbol does a thing with memory.#This is informal and vague.
=The symbol emphasizes the speaker's attachment to memory.#Correct. Emphasizes is academic and accurately used.
~The symbol memories the speaker.#This uses memory incorrectly as a verb.
~The symbol is very stuff about memory.#This is not standard English.
}
::ENG3_U05_L01_Q07::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Draft: The writer uses contrast, to show change. Which revision is correct? {
~The writer uses, contrast to show change.#The comma is incorrectly placed.
=The writer uses contrast to show change.#Correct. No comma is needed between the verb and its object.
~The writer uses contrast, to show, change.#The commas are unnecessary and disruptive.
~The writer, uses contrast to show change.#The comma incorrectly separates the subject and verb.
}
::ENG3_U05_L01_Q08::[MLA.ENG3.W.04] Draft: The article talks about public art and it is important. Which revision improves clarity? {
~The article talks about public art being important and things.#This remains vague.
~Public art is important in the article.#This is clearer but still general.
~The article is about important art in public.#This is awkward.
=The article explains how public art preserves neighborhood history.#Correct. This is clear and specific.
}
::ENG3_U05_L01_Q09::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Draft: The essay begins with silence it then gives examples of public speech. Which revision is correct? {
=The essay begins with silence; it then gives examples of public speech.#Correct. The semicolon joins closely related complete thoughts.
~The essay begins with silence it then gives examples of public speech.#This is a run-on.
~The essay beginning with silence and then examples.#This is a fragment.
~The essay begins with silence, it then gives examples of public speech.#This is a comma splice.
}
::ENG3_U05_L01_Q10::[MLA.ENG3.R.10] Passage: The report argues that libraries function as civic infrastructure. Which paraphrase is clearest? {
~The report says libraries do infrastructure things.#This is vague and informal.
=The report claims that libraries serve important public needs.#Correct. This accurately restates the meaning.
~The report says roads are libraries.#This misreads infrastructure.
~The report argues against libraries.#This contradicts the sentence.
}
::ENG3_U05_L01_Q11::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Draft: Analyzing the mural carefully. What is the best correction? {
~Because analyzing the mural carefully.#This is still a fragment.
~Analyzing carefully the mural.#This is awkward and incomplete.
=The student analyzes the mural carefully.#Correct. This sentence has a subject, verb, and complete thought.
~The mural carefully analyzing.#This is not standard English.
}
::ENG3_U05_L01_Q12::[MLA.ENG3.W.04] Draft: The author uses examples and it helps. Which revision best improves style? {
~The author uses examples and they are helpful.#This is still general.
~Examples are used by the author and help.#This is awkward and vague.
~The author uses examples in a good way.#This is informal.
=The author uses examples to clarify the subject for readers.#Correct. This explains purpose clearly.
}
::ENG3_U05_L01_Q13::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Context: Choose the sentence that is punctuated correctly. {
~The speaker repeats the phrase, because it creates emphasis.#The comma before because is unnecessary here.
=The speaker repeats the phrase because it creates emphasis.#Correct. The sentence is complete and correctly punctuated.
~The speaker repeats, the phrase because it creates emphasis.#This comma separates the verb from its object.
~The speaker, repeats the phrase because it creates emphasis.#This comma separates the subject and verb.
}
::ENG3_U05_L01_Q14::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Draft: The essay shows the subject. Which revision uses a stronger academic verb? {
~The essay has the subject.#This is weaker and vague.
~The essay talks subject.#This is not standard English.
=The essay develops the subject through examples and explanation.#Correct. Develops is precise academic vocabulary.
~The essay is about the subject stuff.#This is informal.
}
::ENG3_U05_L01_Q15::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Draft: The source is credible it gives evidence from interviews. Which revision is correct? {
~The source is credible, it gives evidence from interviews.#This is a comma splice.
~The source credible because interviews.#This is not a complete sentence.
~Because the source is credible and gives evidence from interviews.#This is a fragment.
=The source is credible because it gives evidence from interviews.#Correct. This is complete and clear.
}
::ENG3_U05_L01_Q16::[MLA.ENG3.W.04] Draft: The paragraph is confusing because the sentence boundaries are unclear. What revision step should come first? {
~Add more unrelated evidence.#This would not fix sentence boundaries.
=Identify where each complete thought begins and ends.#Correct. Sentence-boundary revision begins by finding complete thoughts.
~Replace every comma with a period.#This may create fragments or choppy writing.
~Remove all academic vocabulary.#This would weaken analysis.
}
::ENG3_U05_L01_Q17::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Draft: The image appears in every section, it creates unity. Which revision is best? {
=The image appears in every section, and it creates unity.#Correct. The comma plus coordinating conjunction fixes the comma splice.
~The image appears in every section, it creates unity.#This remains a comma splice.
~Because the image appears in every section and creates unity.#This is a fragment.
~The image appearing in every section creates unity, and.#This is incomplete.
}
::ENG3_U05_L01_Q18::[MLA.ENG3.R.10] Source sentence: Repetition can make an argument memorable by giving listeners a pattern to follow. Which paraphrase is strongest? {
~Repetition makes listeners follow patterns and memory by arguing.#This is unclear.
~Arguments are always memorable when repeated.#This overstates the source.
~Listeners only understand repeated arguments.#This adds an unsupported limitation.
=Repeated wording can help listeners remember an argument because it creates a pattern.#Correct. This is accurate and clear.
}
::ENG3_U05_L01_Q19::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Context: Choose the sentence that uses capitalization correctly. {
~The essay describes community Gardens as cultural spaces.#Gardens should not be capitalized here.
~The Essay describes community gardens as cultural spaces.#Essay should not be capitalized here.
=The essay describes community gardens as cultural spaces.#Correct. Only the first word is capitalized.
~the essay describes community gardens as cultural spaces.#The sentence must begin with a capital letter.
}
::ENG3_U05_L01_Q20::[MLA.ENG3.W.04] Draft: The claim is unclear and has a grammar mistake. Which revision goal is best? {
~Make the claim longer without changing meaning.#Length alone will not fix clarity.
~Add slang so the sentence sounds natural.#Slang does not fit academic analysis.
=Revise for both clear meaning and correct conventions.#Correct. The issue involves clarity and grammar.
~Remove the claim from the paragraph.#The claim is needed.
}
::ENG3_U05_L01_Q21::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Draft: The report uses headings, they guide readers through the services. Which revision is correct? {
~The report uses headings, they guide readers through the services.#This is a comma splice.
=The report uses headings to guide readers through the services.#Correct. This revision is concise and grammatical.
~The report using headings to guide readers.#This is a fragment.
~The report uses headings they guide readers.#This is a run-on.
}
::ENG3_U05_L01_Q22::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Context: Choose the sentence that uses clarify correctly. {
~The source clarify the symbol.#The subject and verb do not agree.
~The source is clarify about the symbol.#This is not standard English.
~The source clarifying the symbol.#This is a fragment.
=The source clarifies the symbol's role in the passage.#Correct. Clarifies is used accurately.
}
::ENG3_U05_L01_Q23::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Draft: The paragraph explains the source but it does not connect to the claim. Which punctuation is correct if the sentence keeps both clauses? {
~The paragraph explains the source but, it does not connect to the claim.#The comma is in the wrong place.
=The paragraph explains the source, but it does not connect to the claim.#Correct. A comma before but joins two independent clauses.
~The paragraph explains, the source but it does not connect to the claim.#The comma separates verb and object.
~The paragraph, explains the source but it does not connect to the claim.#The comma separates subject and verb.
}
::ENG3_U05_L01_Q24::[MLA.ENG3.W.04] Draft: This evidence is good because it shows a thing. Which revision is most specific? {
~This evidence is good because it shows something meaningful.#This remains vague.
~This evidence is strong and important.#This is too general.
=This evidence is effective because it shows how the stopped clock symbolizes frozen memory.#Correct. This is specific and analytical.
~This evidence has a thing about memory.#This is informal.
}
::ENG3_U05_L01_Q25::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Draft: The student revised the paragraph, the sentence boundaries became clearer. Which revision is best? {
~The student revised the paragraph, the sentence boundaries became clearer.#This is a comma splice.
~Revising the paragraph, the sentence boundaries clearer.#This is a fragment.
~The student revised the paragraph the sentence boundaries became clearer.#This is a run-on.
=The student revised the paragraph, so the sentence boundaries became clearer.#Correct. The conjunction so shows the relationship between ideas.
}
